Protocol: PhotoPrint_W5

# Week 5: TLS pseudonymous channel variant.
#
# In our scenario:
#   - idp has a public key and acts as a TLS server  => A can open [A] *->* idp
#   - P has a public key and acts as a TLS server    => A can open [A] *->* P
#   - B has a public key and acts as a TLS server    => P can open [P] *->* B
#
# With TLS (server-only auth), the channel [A] *->* idp means:
#   - A knows she is talking to idp (server authenticated via pk(idp))
#   - idp does NOT know who A is until A authenticates at the application layer

Types:
    Agent A, P, B, idp;
    Number Grant, Na, Np;
    Function pwd, photos;
    Format f_auth, f_grant, f_req, f_photos;

Knowledge:
    A:   A, P, B, idp, pk(idp), pk(B), pk(P), pwd(A);
    idp: A, P, B, idp, pk(idp), inv(pk(idp)), pk(P), pk(B), pwd(A);
    P:   A, P, B, idp, pk(idp), pk(P), inv(pk(P)), pk(B);
    B:   A, P, B, idp, pk(idp), pk(B), inv(pk(B)), pk(P), photos(A)

Actions:
    # Step 1: A authenticates to idp over a TLS channel (idp is TLS server).
    # [A] *->* idp replaces the outer pk(idp) encryption.
    # pwd(A) and Na travel in plaintext within the secure channel.
    [A]  *->* idp: f_auth(A, P, B, pwd(A), Na)

    # Step 2: idp issues the signed Grant. This message travels back to A
    # over the same TLS session. The signature lets A, P, and B verify
    # it independently.
    idp -> A:   {f_grant(A, P, B, Grant, Na)}inv(pk(idp))

    # Step 3: A forwards the signed Grant to P over a TLS channel to P.
    # The outer pk(P) encryption is replaced by [A]*->*P.
    # The inner idp signature is kept: P must verify it came from idp.
    [A]  *->* P: {f_grant(A, P, B, Grant, Na)}inv(pk(idp))

    # Step 4: P presents the Grant to B over a TLS channel to B.
    # The outer pk(B) encryption is replaced by [P]*->*B.
    # The inner idp signature is kept for B to verify.
    [P]  *->* B: f_req({f_grant(A, P, B, Grant, Na)}inv(pk(idp)), Np)

    # Step 5: B returns the photos to P over the same TLS session.
    # Since [P]*->*B was established in step 4, B can reply confidentially.
    # The outer pk(P) encryption is replaced by the channel reply direction.
    B    *->* [P]: f_photos(photos(A), Np)

Goals:
    Grant secret between A, idp, P, B;
    photos(A) secret between B, P;
    pwd(A) secret between A, idp;
    P authenticates A on Grant;
    B authenticates A on Grant;
    P authenticates B on Np


# =============================================================================
# Special Task — Week 5: Pseudonymous Channel Variant
# =============================================================================
#
# The task asks us to replace as much cryptography as possible with
# pseudonymous channel notation and to explain the modeling.
#
# Summary of replacements made:
#
# 1. Step 1 was {f_auth(A,P,B,pwd(A),Na)}pk(idp)
#    => Replaced by [A] *->* idp : f_auth(A,P,B,pwd(A),Na)
#    Justification: TLS with server-only auth gives exactly a
#    pseudonymous secure channel to the server (idp). The outer
#    asymmetric encryption pk(idp) is subsumed by the TLS encryption.
#
# 2. Step 2 remains {f_grant(...)}inv(pk(idp))  [NOT replaced]
#    Justification: The signature is a transferable credential that
#    A, P, and B all need to verify independently. A TLS channel only
#    protects the transmission; it does not produce a verifiable token
#    that can be forwarded. The signature must stay.
#
# 3. Step 3 was {{f_grant(...)}inv(pk(idp))}pk(P)
#    => Replaced by [A] *->* P : {f_grant(...)}inv(pk(idp))
#    Justification: The outer pk(P) encryption is replaced by the
#    TLS channel to P. The inner idp signature is kept (see point 2).
#
# 4. Step 4 was {f_req({f_grant(...)}inv(pk(idp)), Np)}pk(B)
#    => Replaced by [P] *->* B : f_req({f_grant(...)}inv(pk(idp)), Np)
#    Justification: The pk(B) encryption is replaced by the TLS
#    channel to B. The inner idp signature is kept.
#
# 5. Step 5 was {f_photos(photos(A), Np)}pk(P)
#    => Replaced by B *->* [P] : f_photos(photos(A), Np)
#    Justification: The pk(P) encryption is replaced by B's reply
#    on the established TLS session. B is authenticated (TLS server),
#    P is pseudonymous (TLS client).
#
# Remaining cryptography: only the idp signature {f_grant(...)}inv(pk(idp))
# and pwd(A) (which is an application-level credential, not a channel key).
#
# Caveat: as noted in the slides, pseudonymous channels do NOT provide
# freshness. Our nonces Na and Np remain necessary to prevent replay attacks.